01:24:37.000 --> 01:25:27.000
there are definite things that people can say that during bereavement that grieving people find annoying or unhelpful. And they run the gamut from, you know, you'll be fine or, you know, I'm thinking about you or whatever that they might say. And in fact, I think Colin's point, and was very well put, and I embrace it. I think that, in fact, what bereaved people, I think probably most would value is somebody who's just simply there to talk with them at whatever level. A great example of this was actually, I was writing a paper. I was doing a research study with a brilliant woman, a very wonderful woman named Lisa Kapps, who was a linguist at Berkeley, and she was given a cancer diagnosis at a terribly young age

139
01:25:27.000 --> 01:25:55.000
and was going to die in six months. We were doing a research study at the time on bereavement, and we were doing it remotely over the phone in those days. And she said to me, you know, George, I would really like to just talk about the research when we meet. I don't want to talk about my cancer. I don't want to talk about facing death. I just want to talk about the research. And she had about six months to live.

